abstract: >-
  We study when a reduced description preserves the strength and singularities of
  the response to an applied source. For systems defined by state updates and
  observations, eliminating variables changes the feedback, source coupling,
  readout and direct response together. For jointly C¹ updates and observations
  and bounded surjective linear coarse maps between Banach spaces, fibre
  consistency yields differentiable descent of the update and observation, and
  hence preservation of their joint source response on the common invertible
  domain. For approximate descent, we derive an exact response-error identity that
  separates defects in the update, source, readout and direct term without
  requiring an inverse coarse map. Resolvent and contour estimates propagate
  reference-branch, source and readout errors to visible poles and their residues
  at displaced critical points. In a reciprocal Hilbert-space realisation with a
  self-adjoint endpoint operator, an isolated finite-dimensional critical
  subspace, a bounded complementary inverse and a positive crossing matrix, the
  visible residue is positive semidefinite and finite rank; source overlap
  determines which critical directions remain visible. Arithmetic and
  reflecting-heat examples distinguish static response preservation from
  memoryless dynamical closure. In the two-mode example, at fixed geometric memory
  decay 0<d<1, retaining m terms gives pole displacement O(d^m) and residue error
  O(md^m). In a vacuum quantum-field-theory realisation, when the exact
  unfiltered-covariance, projection, source-pairing, continuation, subtraction and
  endpoint matching hypotheses hold, the response residue is the zero-mass atom of
  the corresponding positive spectral measure.
keywords:
  - "Coarse-graining"
  - "Generated systems"
  - "Model reduction"
  - "Linear response"
  - "Schur complements"
  - "Memory kernels"
